{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgDafASKhduyPeugkDeCV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019n5P59PeCBsjahXyHLfk4G","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"P=/Users/brenn/.cache/uv/builds-v0/.tmpR1cD4H/bin/python; $P - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('references/checks.json'))\nfor it in [\"B01\",\"B02\",\"B03\",\"B05\"]:\n    print(it, json.dumps(d[it])[:3000]); print()\nE\nsed -n 1,80p scripts/review_coverage.py | head -5; $P scripts/review_coverage.py --help | head -30"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4022,"cache_read_input_tokens":89353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4022},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11970af5-78fd-41cf-a5cf-b78b5394ec79","uuid":"5e4260aa-bea1-4fe0-9d3f-db61e2557014","timestamp":"2026-10-20T13:38:31.097Z","request_id":"req_011CgDafA6V5qcbNZNLZq5Qz","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019n5P59PeCBsjahXyHLfk4G":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-sew8ptyv/skill && P=/Users/brenn/.cache/uv/builds-v0/.tmpR1cD4H/bin/python; $P - <<'E'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('references/checks.json'))\nfor it in [\"B01\",\"B02\",\"B03\",\"B05\"]:\n    print(it, json.dumps(d[it])[:3000]); print()\nE\nsed -n 1,80p scripts/review_coverage.py | head -5; $P scripts/review_coverage.py --help | head -30"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_019n5P59PeCBsjahXyHLfk4G":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-sew8ptyv/skill"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019n5P59PeCBsjahXyHLfk4G","type":"tool_result","content":"B01 {\"gate\": \"G1\", \"title\": \"Minimal tool surface\", \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\", \"requirement\": \"List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \\\"read-only.\\\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.\", \"not_covered\": [{\"problem\": \"A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly\", \"owner\": \"E06\"}, {\"problem\": \"A token or credential with too much access\", \"owner\": \"B02\"}, {\"problem\": \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema\", \"owner\": \"E04\"}, {\"problem\": \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\", \"owner\": \"E03\"}, {\"problem\": \"Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers\", \"owner\": \"E05\"}], \"na_when\": \"never\", \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B01.1\", \"check\": \"Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list.\"}, {\"id\": \"B01.2\", \"check\": \"Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it.\"}, {\"id\": \"B01.3\", \"check\": \"The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues.\"}, {\"id\": \"B01.4\", \"check\": \"Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval\n\nB02 {\"gate\": \"G1\", \"title\": \"Least-privilege access\", \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\", \"requirement\": \"Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Identify the acting agent separately from the original user or system whose authority it uses. Explicitly authorized, narrowly scoped user delegation is valid; copying a login session or inheriting a person's full permissions is not. Test that an allowed read works and an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and another tenant's data all fail. Limit each access token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard grants.\", \"not_covered\": [{\"problem\": \"A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user\", \"owner\": \"R04\"}, {\"problem\": \"Long-lived or static secrets that never expire or rotate\", \"owner\": \"B03\"}, {\"problem\": \"Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens\", \"owner\": \"B04\"}, {\"problem\": \"A sub-agent getting powers beyond its task, or a parent escalating through a peer\", \"owner\": \"E08\"}, {\"problem\": \"Memory readable across tenants or tasks\", \"owner\": \"R05\"}], \"na_when\": \"never\", \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B02.1\", \"check\": \"The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo.\"}, {\"id\": \"B02.2\", \"check\": \"Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query.\"}, {\"id\": \"B02.3\", \"check\": \"Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts an unrelated audience or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo.\"}, {\"id\": \"B02.4\", \"check\": \"The acting agent is verifiably identified separately f\n\nB03 {\"gate\": \"G2\", \"title\": \"Credential lifetime and handling\", \"lead_role\": \"infrastructure\", \"requirement\": \"Issue short-lived credentials from a trusted issuer or managed identity service. For API calls, use expiring access tokens with a specific destination audience, identifying the agent and any explicitly delegated user or system authority, rather than static API keys or personal access tokens. A secrets vault stores backing secrets; it does not itself make a static key into an expiring access token. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where supported, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. If only static keys are supported, isolate them at the enforcement layer, limit their permissions, and record the lifetime and revocation gap. Show expiry and rotation, including allowed overlap, and explain the chosen lifetime.\", \"not_covered\": [{\"problem\": \"A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed\", \"owner\": \"B02\"}, {\"problem\": \"No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user\", \"owner\": \"B04\"}, {\"problem\": \"Secrets showing up in tool output, responses, or normal logs\", \"owner\": \"R03\"}, {\"problem\": \"Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked\", \"owner\": \"B10\"}], \"na_when\": \"never\", \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B03.1\", \"check\": \"Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\"}, {\"id\": \"B03.2\", \"check\": \"Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\"}, {\"id\": \"B03.3\", \"check\": \"No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an ima\n\nB05 {\"gate\": \"G1\", \"title\": \"Destructive-action interception\", \"lead_role\": \"agent_team\", \"requirement\": \"List the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.\", \"not_covered\": [{\"problem\": \"Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail\", \"owner\": \"R13\"}, {\"problem\": \"Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry\", \"owner\": \"B06\"}, {\"problem\": \"Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped\", \"owner\": \"R12\"}, {\"problem\": \"A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft\", \"owner\": \"R09\"}, {\"problem\": \"Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions\", \"owner\": \"R02\"}], \"na_when\": \"never\", \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B05.1\", \"check\": \"High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention.\", \"coverage_required\": true, \"verify\": \"1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or